{:schema 1
 :date "2026-08-14"
 :receipt/type :representation-experiment
 :follows "2026-08-14-representation-ceiling.edn"

 :question
 "The ceiling receipt found a direct index walk 40x faster than the engine and
  attributed the gap to three things: a persistent-map binding per row, an
  {:s :p :o} map per candidate, and a set at every step. Price them."

 :answer
 "Those three, together, are 3.0x of a 33.9x gap. The remaining 11.4x is
  something else. The attribution in the previous receipt was wrong."

 :method
 "Arm B0 from the ceiling receipt, with one general-engine feature added back at
  a time. Every arm asserted to return the same set as the engine, on all five
  start persons, before anything was timed. 10 warmup rounds, 60 samples, median.
  Host load 4.82."

 :arms
 [{:arm "B0" :what "positional loop, vector out, no visible?, no dedup" :ms 0.527}
  {:arm "B1" :what "+ visible? applied to every candidate quad" :ms 0.562 :vs-previous "1.07x"}
  {:arm "B2" :what "+ a set at every step (deduplication)" :ms 0.940 :vs-previous "1.67x"}
  {:arm "B3" :what "+ a persistent-map binding per row, projected at the end" :ms 1.568 :vs-previous "1.67x"}
  {:arm "A"  :what "the engine, dl/q" :ms 17.853 :vs-previous "11.39x"}]

 :the-reading
 "Visibility costs almost nothing -- 7%. Deduplication costs 1.67x. Map-shaped
  bindings cost another 1.67x. All three are real and all three together are 3.0x.
  The engine is 11.4x beyond that.

  So the answer to 'how much of the gap does generality need?' is: about a tenth
  of it. The rest is not the price of being a general engine. It is in how this
  engine reaches the index -- arms B0 through B3 all read `:pos` and `:spo`
  directly, while the engine goes through `scan*` and `query-seq`, which
  construct an {:s :p :o} map per candidate and traverse lazy seqs. That layer
  was measured on 2026-08-14 at 131 ns a row against 0.4 ns for the raw index
  read."

 :this-refutes-its-own-predecessor
 "2026-08-14-the-gap-is-a-constant-factor.edn named binding maps, quad maps and
  per-step sets as the 9x, and 2026-08-14-representation-ceiling.edn repeated it
  for the 40x. Two of those three are now priced at 1.67x each and the third at
  1.07x. The claim was plausible, was stated as a reading rather than a
  measurement, and was wrong."

 :caveats
 ["One query shape, one dataset, one JVM. The arms are hand-written for IC02's
   clause order and prove nothing about other shapes."
  "B1 constructs the {:s :p :o} map it passes to visible?, so its 7% includes that
   allocation for three quads per row. A general engine that already has the quad
   would pay less."
  "The 11.4x is a residue, not a measurement of anything named. Calling it
   'scan* and query-seq' is the next hypothesis to test, not a result."]

 :next
 "Price the read path the same way: a walk that reads `:pos`/`:spo` directly
  against one that goes through `query-seq` and materialises {:s :p :o}, same
  query, answers asserted equal. That splits the 11.4x into 'how the engine
  reads' and 'everything else', and it is the last decomposition available before
  the remainder has to be attacked in the engine itself."}
